WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.001
"Are Environmental Toxins Lower
in Wild-Caught or Farmed Fish?"

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.120
Although the levels
of industrial pollutants,

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.800
like dioxins and PCBs,

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.880
continue to decline
in the food supply,

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:14.240
there is one dietary source

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:17.920
that still remains
a major threat: fish.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.560
Everything eventually flows
into the sea.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:23.440
Yes, we can get some dioxins
from eating horses,

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.960
but most of our exposure
comes from eating fish.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:28.960
The U.S. Environmental
Protection Agency

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:31.600
puts the tolerable upper limit
of dioxin intake

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:35.200
per kilogram of body weight
at 0.7 picograms—

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.560
less than one trillionth
of a gram— per day.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:42.400
So, we should get less than this
in our diet every day.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:45.120
As you can see,
we're already skirting the max

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:46.640
by just consuming dairy,

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.200
and fish takes us
right over the top,

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.280
even at low levels
of consumption.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.400
So, the adverse effects
of chemical contaminants

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.640
in seafood may counteract
the benefits

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.720
of any nutrients in fish.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:00.640
So much so
that many dietary guidelines

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.360
recommend no more than
one serving a week of fish

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.760
and seafood to cut down
on exposure

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.840
to toxic pollutants.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.600
But which is worse?
Wild-caught or farmed?

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:13.280
Take salmon for example.

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:17.040
Salmon had the highest toxic
equivalents of PCBs,

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.680
followed by canned tuna,
as well as the highest

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.560
neurotoxic equivalents of PCBs.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.120
If you compare the levels
of PCBs in salmon

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:26.960
farmed in Maine and Canada,

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:29.520
versus salmon
wild-caught in Alaska,

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.200
versus organically farmed salmon
from Norway,

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.960
compared
to the wild-caught salmon,

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:37.360
the farmed salmon,
organic or otherwise,

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:41.280
had significantly higher
PCB levels.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:44.400
This appears to extend
to other contaminants too.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.480
Based on the testing
of literally tons

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:48.880
of salmon samples
from around the world,

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:52.160
for every toxin
the researchers tested,

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:53.920
the samples of farmed salmon

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:57.200
had higher levels than
did the wild-caught salmon—

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.680
higher levels of DDT and
these other banned pesticides.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:05.120
Over 10 times more PCBs
in farmed salmon;

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.920
over 10 times more dioxins.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.640
In order to not exceed
EPA safety levels,

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:12.640
we wouldn't want to eat
supermarket salmon

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.320
more than once every two weeks
in Denver or New Orleans,

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.680
more than once a month in LA,
Vancouver, DC, Seattle,

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.800
Chicago, or New York,
just once every two months

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:27.120
in Edinburgh, Paris,
London, Oslo, Boston,

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:29.200
San Francisco, or Toronto,

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:33.040
and just a few times a year
in Frankfurt, Germany.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:36.080
We can track pollutants
from the ocean to the table,

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:37.520
and it's through the fish oil.

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:40.960
The fish oil used in the feed
for farmed fish

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.600
transfers pollutants
to the fish themselves,

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:46.640
and then the fish carries the
pollutants to human consumers.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:49.680
So, yes, salmon has
the healthy omega-3 fatty acids,

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:52.400
but they also contain
high levels of toxic chemicals,

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.200
and not just the PCBs,
dioxins, and pesticides.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:58.240
Farmed salmon test positive
for antibiotic residues,

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.600
higher levels of forever
chemicals, and flame retardants,

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:05.360
and also test positive for
endocrine disrupting compounds—

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.520
hormone disruptors like BPA.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.320
Something you don't see
in samples of sea vegetables

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:13.760
like seaweed, presumably because
of their low fat content.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.480
Farmed salmon may also
have higher levels of mercury

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:17.680
than wild-caught,

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:20.880
though the opposite may be
the case for farmed tuna.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:23.440
Arsenic also goes both ways,
with more arsenic

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:26.000
in wild seabream
compared to farmed,

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.800
but less in wild seabass.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:31.360
However, all the arsenic levels
were bad,

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:33.120
exceeding
cancer benchmark values

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.040
indicating a moderate risk of cancer
due to fish consumption.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:40.000
Some analyses have
found dioxin and PCB levels

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.400
similar across the board,
but, in general,

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:46.080
you can think of aquaculture
fish as "farmed and dangerous"

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:47.040
compared to wild-caught

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.440
in terms of higher
levels of most pollutants.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:51.760
So, that's why researchers
emphasize the importance

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.160
of labeling as a means
to help consumers

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:57.840
avoid unnecessary exposure
to highly contaminated fish.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.760
Unfortunately,
mislabeling is rampant.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:04.640
So, the farmed versus
wild-caught question

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.680
may be largely academic
given the extent

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.480
of seafood fraud
and mislabeling.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:12.640
An investigation
by the New York Attorney General

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:15.040
found that consumers
who bought what was advertised

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:18.000
as "wild" salmon
actually often received

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.840
farm-raised salmon instead.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:22.000
Up to 43 percent
of salmon tested

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.880
across the United States,
for example, were mislabeled,

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:30.430
most commonly passing off
farmed salmon as wild-caught.

